NNAAMMEE
     rraadd..ccoonnff — router advertisement daemon configuration file

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The rad(8) daemon is an IPv6 router advertisement daemon.

     The rraadd..ccoonnff config file is divided into the following main sections:

     _M_a_c_r_o_s
           Definitions of variables that can be used later, simplifying the
           configuration file.

     _G_l_o_b_a_l _c_o_n_f_i_g_u_r_a_t_i_o_n
           Global settings for rad(8).  These are used as default values for
           iinntteerrffaaccee definitions and can be overwritten in an iinntteerrffaaccee block.

     _I_n_t_e_r_f_a_c_e_s
           rad(8) sends IPv6 router advertisement messages.  This section de‐
           fines on which interfaces to advertise prefix information and their
           associated parameters.

     Additional configuration files can be included with the iinncclluuddee keyword.

   MMaaccrrooss
     A macro is defined with a command of the form _n_a_m_e=_v_a_l_u_e.  The macro _n_a_m_e
     can contain letters, digits, and underscores and cannot be a reserved
     word (for example, iinntteerrffaaccee).  Within unquoted arguments, the string
     $_n_a_m_e is later expanded to _v_a_l_u_e.

   GGlloobbaall ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn
     The global configuration section sets defaults for router advertisement
     messages.  These can be overwritten in interface blocks.

     ddeeffaauulltt rroouutteerr (yyeess|nnoo)
             Act as a default router or not.  The default is yyeess.

     ddnnss {_o_p_t_i_o_n _._._.}
             Add a DNS router advertisement option to communicate network pro‐
             vided nameservers.  In a ddnnss block, a sseeaarrcchh option or at least
             one nnaammeesseerrvveerr option is required, whereas specifying the
             lliiffeettiimmee is optional:

             lliiffeettiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
                     The number of seconds the dns options are valid after re‐
                     ceiving a router advertisement message.  The default is
                     1800 seconds.

             nnaammeesseerrvveerr (_a_d_d_r_e_s_s|{_a_d_d_r_e_s_s _._._.})
                     IPv6 address or list of IPv6 addresses of DNS name
                     servers.

             sseeaarrcchh (_d_o_m_a_i_n|{_d_o_m_a_i_n _._._.})
                     Domain or list of domains for the resolv.conf(5) search
                     list.

     hhoopp lliimmiitt _h_o_p_s
             Specify the diameter of the internet.  The default is 0, meaning
             unspecified by this router.

     mmaannaaggeedd aaddddrreessss ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, indicate that stateless address configuration pre‐
             fixes are not available and hosts should consult DHCPv6.  The de‐
             fault is nnoo.

     mmttuu _b_y_t_e_s
             The MTU option is used in Router Advertisement messages to ensure
             that all nodes on a link use the same MTU value in those cases
             where the link MTU is not well known.  The default is 0, meaning
             unspecified by this router.

     nnaatt6644 pprreeffiixx _p_r_e_f_i_x [{_o_p_t_i_o_n _._._.}]
             Add a PREF64 router advertisement option to communicate prefixes
             used for Network Address and Protocol Translation from IPv6 to
             IPv4 (NAT64).  If _p_r_e_f_i_x is specified without a prefix length,
             its default is 64.

             nnaatt6644 pprreeffiixx options are as follows:

             lliiffeettiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
                     The number of seconds the nat64 prefix option is valid
                     after receiving a router advertisement message.  A value
                     of zero indicates to not use the prefix anymore.  The
                     maximum is 65528 seconds.  The default is 1800 seconds.

     ootthheerr ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn (yyeess|nnoo)
             If set to yyeess, hosts should consult DHCPv6 for additional config‐
             uration like NTP servers or DNS name servers.  The default is nnoo.

     rroouutteerr lliiffeettiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
             The number of seconds this router is a valid default router after
             receiving a router advertisement message.  The default is 1800
             seconds.

     rroouutteerr pprreeffeerreennccee (hhiigghh|mmeeddiiuumm|llooww)
             Indicate whether to prefer this router over other default
             routers.  The default is mmeeddiiuumm.

     rreeaacchhaabbllee ttiimmee _n_u_m_b_e_r
             The time, in milliseconds, that a node assumes a neighbor is
             reachable.  The default is 0, meaning unspecified by this router.
             This option is ignored by slaacd(8) and cannot be set on OpenBSD
             IPv6 hosts.

     rreettrraannss ttiimmeerr _n_u_m_b_e_r
             The time, in milliseconds, between retransmitted Neighbor Solici‐
             tation messages.  The default is 0, meaning unspecified by this
             router.  This option is ignored by slaacd(8) and cannot be set on
             OpenBSD IPv6 hosts.

     ssoouurrccee lliinnkk--llaayyeerr aaddddrreessss (yyeess|nnoo)
             Add a source link-layer address option to router advertisement
             messages, to communicate the link-layer address of the sending
             interface.  The default is yyeess.

   IInntteerrffaacceess
     This section contains a list of interfaces or interface groups to send
     advertisements on.  At least one iinntteerrffaaccee is required.

     iinntteerrffaaccee _n_a_m_e [{_p_r_e_f_i_x _._._.}]
             Options set in the global section can be overwritten inside an
             interface block.  In addition an interface block can contain a
             list of prefixes:

             [nnoo] aauuttoo pprreeffiixx [{_o_p_t_i_o_n _._._.}]
             pprreeffiixx _p_r_e_f_i_x [{_o_p_t_i_o_n _._._.}]
                     The default is to discover prefixes to announce by in‐
                     specting the IPv6 addresses configured on an interface.
                     This can be disabled with nnoo aauuttoo pprreeffiixx.  If _p_r_e_f_i_x is
                     specified without a prefix length, its default is 64.

                     pprreeffiixx options are as follows:

                     aauuttoonnoommoouuss aaddddrreessss--ccoonnffiigguurraattiioonn (yyeess|nnoo)
                             This prefix can be used to generate IPv6 ad‐
                             dresses.  The default is yyeess.

                     oonn--lliinnkk (yyeess|nnoo)
                             This prefix is considered on-link.  The default
                             is yyeess.

                     pprreeffeerrrreedd lliiffeettiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
                             The preferred lifetime (pltime) in seconds for
                             addresses generated from this prefix.  The de‐
                             fault is 2700.  This option is ignored if the
                             prefix is discovered from a network interface and
                             it has a lower preferred lifetime.

                     vvaalliidd lliiffeettiimmee _s_e_c_o_n_d_s
                             The valid lifetime (vltime) in seconds for ad‐
                             dresses generated from this prefix.  The default
                             is 5400.  This option is ignored if the prefix is
                             discovered from a network interface and it has a
                             lower valid lifetime.

